WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.200
 

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:08.200
Ted Hart: When I was 13 years old, I lost my hearing when I got the

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:11.200
mumps.  As you can imagine, this makes it very difficult to

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.200
communicate with people.  When I met my future wife she was

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:17.200
able to communicate with me only by spelling out letters on

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:20.200
my hand.  A month later, we met again, and I found out that

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:24.200
she had taught herself the alphabet.  This was a successful

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:27.200
strategy because we’ve been married now for

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.200
19 years.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:32.200
Will Lewis:  Last fall Ted had come to my office to tell me

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:36.200
about an idea that he had for adapting Skype Translator to a

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:38.200
scenario that would actually be useful to him.  Ted, being

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:41.200
deaf, can't make calls over Skype.  But with Skype

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:44.200
Translator, because it produces transcripts, he could

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:47.200
actually read the response back from someone.  So we sat

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.200
down and talked about it for a little bit and there were

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.200
some pieces missing in Skype Translator.  So we needed

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:55.200
actually do something to make it more appropriate for that

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:56.200
scenario.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:00.200
 

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.200
COMPUTER:  Ted Hart is requesting an automatically translated

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:03.200
call.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.200
Ted Hart:  I usually need to have an ASL interpreter for my

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:08.200
meetings at work.  That means that meetings have to be

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:10.200
planned in advance and sometimes this isn't always possible.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:14.200
It doesn't replace an ASL interpreter but it gives me a lot

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:18.200
of other options if one can't be reached at the last minute.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.200
Will Lewis:  These features were really impactful for Ted in his

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.200
work life.  What really hit me was the realization that

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.200
there was a big impact on his personal life.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.200
Ted Hart:  I realized that using Skype Translator I could call my

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:31.200
wife directly for the first time.  There are relay services that

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:35.200
you can use, but they are inconvenient and require time to

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.200
initiate the call.  With Skype Translator, I just read the

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.200
transcript and I understand what she is saying.  It's really

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.200
cool that 24 years after she learned sign language to enter

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.200
my world, I can take a more full step into hers.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:48.200
 

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:51.200
Jenny Lay-Flurrie:  So I was sitting back thinking about this and

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:54.200
looking at it and realizing the impact that this could have

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:58.200
for me, for people here at Microsoft, and for our customers,

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.200
for people with deafness, with hard of hearing, all over the

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.200
world, technology has that power.  It can empower people to

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:08.200
do things and make things easier.  It's available now.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:12.200
Download it.  Give it a try.  And help us to make this a

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:14.200
better and better product.

